# What's New policy

When you (the AI agent) make a commit whose subject starts with `feat:`, `feature:`, `improve:`, or `enhance:` (a user-facing feature or reconfiguration), you MUST also:

1. Add an entry at **Admin → What's New** in the deployed app (or instruct the user to do so once the change is live). This is what users will see in the login primer.
2. Add a marker file `docs/whats_new/entries/YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md` in the same commit (or same PR), using the template in `docs/whats_new/README.md`. This is the repo-side signal that an entry was added.

CI (`.github/workflows/whats-new-check.yml`) and the local pre-push hook (`scripts/check-whats-new.sh`) enforce this — a feature commit without an accompanying marker file will be rejected.

## When a marker is NOT required

Pure fixes, chores, refactors, docs, tests, style, or perf changes (`fix:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `style:`, `perf:`) do NOT require a marker. If a `fix:` materially changes a user-visible workflow, promote the commit subject to `feat:` or `improve:` so the policy triggers and a marker is added.

## Decision rule

- Subject starts with `feat` / `feature` / `improve` / `enhance` → marker required.
- Anything else → marker not required.
- When in doubt, add a marker — it doubles as a readable changelog entry.

